# 🗂️ State Management

## Principles
- **Start simple**: Use the simplest solution that works
- **Colocate state**: Keep state as close to where it's used as possible
- **Server state ≠ Client state**: Treat them differently
- **URL as source of truth**: For shareable/bookmarkable state
- **Lift state only when necessary**: Don't prematurely optimize

## State Categories

### 1. Server State
**Definition**: Data from the server (users, posts, products)

**Tools**: 
- Server Components (Next.js) - preferred
- SWR / React Query / TanStack Query
- Convex hooks

**Rules**:
- Fetch in Server Components when possible
- Use SWR/Query for client-side data fetching
- Cache aggressively with proper revalidation
- Never store in useState/Context (it's not client state)

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Server Component
async function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const user = await db.user.findUnique({ where: { id: userId } });
  return <ProfileCard user={user} />;
}

// ✅ Good - Client-side with SWR
'use client';
import useSWR from 'swr';

function UserProfile({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const { data: user, error } = useSWR(`/api/users/${userId}`);
  // ... handle loading/error states
}

// ❌ Bad - useState for server data
const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
useEffect(() => {
  fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then(res => setUser(res));
}, [userId]);
```

### 2. URL State
**Definition**: State that should be shareable/bookmarkable (filters, search, pagination)

**Tools**:
- Next.js `useSearchParams` / `useRouter`
- URL query parameters

**Rules**:
- Filters, search, pagination → URL params
- Modal open/closed → URL param or hash
- Tab selection → URL param
- Sort order → URL param

```tsx
// ✅ Good - search in URL
'use client';
import { useRouter, useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation';

function SearchBar() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const searchParams = useSearchParams();
  const query = searchParams.get('q') ?? '';

  const handleSearch = (value: string) => {
    const params = new URLSearchParams(searchParams);
    if (value) {
      params.set('q', value);
    } else {
      params.delete('q');
    }
    router.push(`/search?${params.toString()}`);
  };

  return <input value={query} onChange={(e) => handleSearch(e.target.value)} />;
}

// ❌ Bad - search in useState (not shareable)
const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
```

### 3. Local Component State
**Definition**: State used only within a single component

**Tools**: `useState`, `useReducer`

**Rules**:
- Form inputs (uncontrolled)
- Toggle states (dropdown open/closed)
- Temporary UI state
- Keep it local until you need to share it

```tsx
// ✅ Good - local toggle state
function Dropdown() {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}>Toggle</button>
      {isOpen && <DropdownMenu />}
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - premature Context for local state
const DropdownContext = createContext();
// This is overkill if only one dropdown
```

### 4. Shared Component State
**Definition**: State shared between a few related components

**Tools**: 
- Lift state to common parent
- Context (for deeply nested components)

**Rules**:
- Lift state to the nearest common ancestor
- Use Context only if prop drilling is excessive (>3 levels)
- Keep Context scoped (don't make everything global)

```tsx
// ✅ Good - lift state to parent
function Form() {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});
  
  return (
    <>
      <EmailInput value={formData.email} onChange={(email) => setFormData({...formData, email})} />
      <PasswordInput value={formData.password} onChange={(password) => setFormData({...formData, password})} />
      <SubmitButton data={formData} />
    </>
  );
}

// ✅ Good - Context for deep nesting
const FormContext = createContext();

function Form() {
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});
  
  return (
    <FormContext.Provider value={{ formData, setFormData }}>
      <FormLayout>
        <FormSection>
          <DeepNestedInput /> {/* Can access formData without prop drilling */}
        </FormSection>
      </FormLayout>
    </FormContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

### 5. Global Client State
**Definition**: State needed across many unrelated components

**Tools**:
- Zustand (recommended)
- Jotai
- Context (for simple cases)
- Avoid Redux unless you have specific needs

**Rules**:
- Use for true global state (user session, theme, UI preferences)
- Don't use for server state
- Keep it minimal

```tsx
// ✅ Good - Zustand for global state
import { create } from 'zustand';

const useAuthStore = create((set) => ({
  user: null,
  setUser: (user) => set({ user }),
  logout: () => set({ user: null }),
}));

// Use anywhere
function Header() {
  const { user, logout } = useAuthStore();
  return <button onClick={logout}>Logout {user?.name}</button>;
}

// ❌ Bad - Context for complex global state
// Context causes re-renders of all consumers when any part changes
```

### 6. Form State
**Definition**: Form inputs, validation, submission

**Tools**:
- React Hook Form (recommended)
- Server Actions (Next.js)
- Uncontrolled inputs with FormData

**Rules**:
- Prefer uncontrolled inputs with React Hook Form
- Use Server Actions for form submission
- Client-side validation + server-side validation
- Don't manage every input in useState

```tsx
// ✅ Good - React Hook Form
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod';

function SignupForm() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm({
    resolver: zodResolver(signupSchema),
  });

  const onSubmit = async (data) => {
    await createUser(data); // Server Action
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      <input {...register('email')} />
      {errors.email && <p>{errors.email.message}</p>}
      <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
    </form>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - manual state for every field
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
const [password, setPassword] = useState('');
const [emailError, setEmailError] = useState('');
// ...too much boilerplate
```

## Decision Tree

```
Is this state from the server (users, posts, etc.)?
├─ Yes → Use Server Components or SWR/React Query
└─ No → Continue

Should this state be shareable via URL (filters, search)?
├─ Yes → Use URL params (useSearchParams)
└─ No → Continue

Is this state used only in one component?
├─ Yes → Use useState
└─ No → Continue

Is this state shared between a few nearby components?
├─ Yes → Lift to common parent
└─ No → Continue

Is this state needed across many unrelated components?
├─ Yes → Use Zustand or Context
└─ No → Re-evaluate if you actually need state management
```

## Common Patterns

### Derived State
Don't store what you can calculate:

```tsx
// ✅ Good - derive state
function ShoppingCart({ items }) {
  const total = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
  const itemCount = items.length;
  
  return <div>Total: ${total} ({itemCount} items)</div>;
}

// ❌ Bad - duplicate state
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
const [total, setTotal] = useState(0);
const [itemCount, setItemCount] = useState(0);
// Now you have to keep these in sync
```

### Optimistic Updates
Update UI immediately, rollback on error:

```tsx
// ✅ Good - optimistic with SWR
import useSWR, { mutate } from 'swr';

function LikeButton({ postId }) {
  const { data } = useSWR(`/api/posts/${postId}`);
  
  const handleLike = async () => {
    // Optimistically update UI
    mutate(
      `/api/posts/${postId}`,
      { ...data, likes: data.likes + 1 },
      false // don't revalidate yet
    );
    
    try {
      await likePost(postId);
      // Revalidate to get server state
      mutate(`/api/posts/${postId}`);
    } catch (error) {
      // Rollback on error
      mutate(`/api/posts/${postId}`);
    }
  };
  
  return <button onClick={handleLike}>Like ({data?.likes})</button>;
}
```

### Computed Values (useMemo)
Memoize expensive calculations:

```tsx
// ✅ Good - memoize expensive computation
const sortedAndFilteredUsers = useMemo(() => {
  return users
    .filter(user => user.active)
    .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
}, [users]);

// ❌ Bad - recalculate on every render
const sortedAndFilteredUsers = users
  .filter(user => user.active)
  .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
```

### State Colocation
Keep state close to where it's used:

```tsx
// ✅ Good - state colocated with usage
function ExpandableSection() {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setIsExpanded(!isExpanded)}>Toggle</button>
      {isExpanded && <Content />}
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ Bad - state far from usage
function ParentComponent() {
  const [section1Expanded, setSection1Expanded] = useState(false);
  const [section2Expanded, setSection2Expanded] = useState(false);
  const [section3Expanded, setSection3Expanded] = useState(false);
  
  return (
    <>
      <ExpandableSection expanded={section1Expanded} setExpanded={setSection1Expanded} />
      <ExpandableSection expanded={section2Expanded} setExpanded={setSection2Expanded} />
      <ExpandableSection expanded={section3Expanded} setExpanded={setSection3Expanded} />
    </>
  );
}
```

## Anti-Patterns

### ❌ Using Context for Everything
```tsx
// Bad - massive Context with unrelated state
const AppContext = createContext();

function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState();
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState();
  const [notifications, setNotifications] = useState([]);
  const [cart, setCart] = useState([]);
  // ... everything
  
  // Every update re-renders ALL consumers
}

// ✅ Good - separate contexts
const UserContext = createContext();
const ThemeContext = createContext();
const NotificationsContext = createContext();
```

### ❌ Storing Server Data in useState
```tsx
// Bad - manual fetch and state management
const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);

useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/api/users').then(res => setUsers(res));
}, []);

// ✅ Good - use server state library
const { data: users } = useSWR('/api/users');
```

### ❌ Prop Drilling Instead of Composition
```tsx
// Bad - drilling props through multiple levels
<Parent>
  <Child user={user} theme={theme} onAction={handleAction}>
    <GrandChild user={user} theme={theme} onAction={handleAction}>
      <GreatGrandChild user={user} theme={theme} onAction={handleAction} />
    </GrandChild>
  </Child>
</Parent>

// ✅ Good - component composition
<Parent>
  <Child>
    <GrandChild>
      <GreatGrandChild /> {/* Gets what it needs from Context or props */}
    </GrandChild>
  </GrandChild>
  </Child>
</Parent>
```

## Quick Reference

| State Type | Tool | Example |
|------------|------|---------|
| Server data | Server Components, SWR | Users, posts, products |
| URL state | useSearchParams | Filters, search, pagination |
| Local UI | useState | Dropdown open, form input |
| Shared nearby | Lift state up | Form data across fields |
| Global client | Zustand | User session, theme |
| Form | React Hook Form | Complex forms with validation |
| Derived | useMemo | Computed values from other state |

**Golden Rule**: Use the least powerful tool that solves your problem. Start with useState, only add complexity when necessary.
